1
00:00:02,950 --> 00:00:07,907
In 2012, one of America's oldest department
store chains attempted something

2
00:00:07,947 --> 00:00:08,977
extraordinary.

3
00:00:09,618 --> 00:00:12,393
It tried to break its customers'
addiction to discounts.

4
00:00:13,151 --> 00:00:16,470
No endless coupons. No
confusing markdowns.

5
00:00:16,780 --> 00:00:18,770
No constant promotional theater.

6
00:00:19,300 --> 00:00:24,032
Instead, JCPenney would offer
simpler, more predictable prices.

7
00:00:24,703 --> 00:00:29,390
The idea came from an executive who
had helped transform retail at Apple.

8
00:00:29,868 --> 00:00:34,548
But within one fiscal year, JCPenney's
annual sales had fallen by more than four

9
00:00:34,588 --> 00:00:35,618
billion dollars.

10
00:00:36,557 --> 00:00:41,063
How could a strategy designed to make
shopping easier produce such a devastating

11
00:00:41,103 --> 00:00:42,103
result?

12
00:00:43,650 --> 00:00:47,745
Ron Johnson arrived at JCPenney
with an extraordinary reputation.

13
00:00:48,462 --> 00:00:53,378
He had led Apple's retail operations,
helping establish a store experience that

14
00:00:53,418 --> 00:00:55,988
became one of the most
admired in the industry.

15
00:00:56,399 --> 00:00:58,785
Before Apple, he had worked at Target.

16
00:00:59,413 --> 00:01:03,916
In November 2011, he became
JCPenney's chief executive.

17
00:01:04,450 --> 00:01:07,274
The company was struggling
to remain relevant.

18
00:01:07,876 --> 00:01:11,285
Johnson wasn't hired to
preserve the status quo.

19
00:01:11,325 --> 00:01:13,429
He was hired to transform it.

20
00:01:13,784 --> 00:01:18,641
And his first major target was something
deeply embedded in the department store

21
00:01:18,681 --> 00:01:19,681
business.

22
00:01:20,291 --> 00:01:22,058
The way merchandise was priced.

23
00:01:23,876 --> 00:01:27,885
JCPenney had been part of
American retail since 1902.

24
00:01:28,545 --> 00:01:33,810
Generations of shoppers knew its department
stores, clothing, household goods, and

25
00:01:33,850 --> 00:01:35,580
familiar promotional events.

26
00:01:36,221 --> 00:01:40,879
But by the early twenty-first century,
traditional department stores faced growing

27
00:01:40,919 --> 00:01:41,919
pressure.

28
00:01:42,154 --> 00:01:44,179
Competition was intensifying.

29
00:01:44,632 --> 00:01:46,851
Customers had more places to shop.

30
00:01:47,365 --> 00:01:51,713
And JCPenney had become heavily
dependent on promotional activity.

31
00:01:52,316 --> 00:01:55,108
The company needed a reason
for shoppers to choose it.

32
00:01:55,470 --> 00:01:58,937
Johnson believed the answer
was not another coupon.

33
00:01:59,533 --> 00:02:02,345
It was a completely different
shopping experience.

34
00:02:04,273 --> 00:02:09,457
In 2011, JCPenney ran more than five
hundred and ninety promotional events.

35
00:02:10,001 --> 00:02:16,098
For customers, that meant an almost constant
stream of sales, offers, and markdowns.

36
00:02:16,633 --> 00:02:20,087
But for management, the system
created a different problem.

37
00:02:20,630 --> 00:02:26,096
If merchandise was always on sale, what
did the original price actually mean?

38
00:02:26,647 --> 00:02:30,796
Johnson believed customers would welcome
a more straightforward approach.

39
00:02:31,126 --> 00:02:35,875
Instead of navigating a complicated
promotional calendar, they could shop with

40
00:02:35,915 --> 00:02:36,915
confidence.

41
00:02:37,304 --> 00:02:40,609
There was one crucial
assumption behind that idea.

42
00:02:41,052 --> 00:02:45,519
That customers valued a simpler price more
than the feeling of getting a bargain.

43
00:02:47,204 --> 00:02:53,576
On January twenty-fifth, 2012, JCPenney
unveiled its transformation plan.

44
00:02:53,616 --> 00:02:56,998
The new pricing system was
called Fair and Square.

45
00:02:57,533 --> 00:02:59,513
It introduced three categories.

46
00:03:00,145 --> 00:03:01,179
Everyday prices.

47
00:03:01,872 --> 00:03:03,178
Month-long values.

48
00:03:03,777 --> 00:03:08,010
And the company's lowest prices,
offered at designated times.

49
00:03:08,561 --> 00:03:11,491
The new strategy began on February first.

50
00:03:11,773 --> 00:03:15,385
JCPenney was not eliminating
every promotion.

51
00:03:15,808 --> 00:03:20,694
But it was abandoning the constant cycle of
coupons and temporary markdowns that had

52
00:03:20,734 --> 00:03:23,504
defined its relationship
with many customers.

53
00:03:24,131 --> 00:03:26,230
The company was changing more than prices.

54
00:03:26,890 --> 00:03:29,868
It was changing how
shoppers understood value.

55
00:03:31,521 --> 00:03:34,013
On paper, the logic was compelling.

56
00:03:34,666 --> 00:03:37,086
Simpler prices could reduce confusion.

57
00:03:37,692 --> 00:03:42,551
Customers wouldn't need to wait for a coupon
or wonder whether a sale was genuine.

58
00:03:43,136 --> 00:03:48,060
The business could spend less energy
creating promotional events and more energy

59
00:03:48,100 --> 00:03:50,750
improving its merchandise and stores.

60
00:03:51,384 --> 00:03:56,151
Johnson also envisioned a new retail
environment built around individual branded

61
00:03:56,191 --> 00:03:57,191
shops.

62
00:03:57,509 --> 00:03:59,088
The ambition was enormous.

63
00:03:59,772 --> 00:04:03,775
But a successful strategy requires
more than internal logic.

64
00:04:04,300 --> 00:04:08,643
It must fit the behavior of the
people who actually buy the product.

65
00:04:09,106 --> 00:04:12,181
And JCPenney was about to
discover that distinction.

66
00:04:14,022 --> 00:04:15,633
Imagine two shirts.

67
00:04:16,233 --> 00:04:18,559
One has a regular price of forty dollars.

68
00:04:19,409 --> 00:04:23,054
The other is marked down
from sixty dollars to forty.

69
00:04:23,441 --> 00:04:25,992
The customer pays exactly the same amount.

70
00:04:26,528 --> 00:04:29,483
But the second shirt appears
to offer a bigger bargain.

71
00:04:29,979 --> 00:04:33,535
That difference is a familiar
feature of retail psychology.

72
00:04:34,148 --> 00:04:37,863
A reference price can influence
how attractive an offer feels.

73
00:04:38,403 --> 00:04:41,878
JCPenney's new approach
challenged that habit.

74
00:04:42,277 --> 00:04:46,818
It asked shoppers to trust the everyday
price without the reassurance of a dramatic

75
00:04:46,858 --> 00:04:47,868
markdown.

76
00:04:48,309 --> 00:04:52,568
And for customers accustomed to
promotions, that was a major change.

77
00:04:54,485 --> 00:04:56,493
The early results were troubling.

78
00:04:56,939 --> 00:05:01,006
Customers did not embrace the
transformation at the scale management had

79
00:05:01,046 --> 00:05:02,046
anticipated.

80
00:05:02,417 --> 00:05:04,074
Sales weakened sharply.

81
00:05:04,614 --> 00:05:09,851
JCPenney adjusted its messaging and
promotional approach as the year progressed.

82
00:05:10,541 --> 00:05:15,656
But the company was simultaneously changing
prices, marketing, merchandise, and the

83
00:05:15,696 --> 00:05:16,746
store experience.

84
00:05:17,594 --> 00:05:20,545
That made the turnaround
unusually difficult.

85
00:05:21,012 --> 00:05:25,603
If customers stopped visiting, which part
of the transformation was responsible?

86
00:05:26,266 --> 00:05:27,266
The pricing?

87
00:05:27,445 --> 00:05:28,445
The products?

88
00:05:28,704 --> 00:05:30,069
The advertising?

89
00:05:30,258 --> 00:05:32,610
Or the pace of change itself?

90
00:05:33,274 --> 00:05:37,706
The financial results would soon make
the problem impossible to ignore.

91
00:05:39,459 --> 00:05:45,856
In fiscal 2011, JCPenney reported seventeen
point two six billion dollars in net

92
00:05:45,896 --> 00:05:46,896
sales.

93
00:05:47,142 --> 00:05:52,321
In fiscal 2012, that figure fell
to twelve point nine nine billion.

94
00:05:52,871 --> 00:05:56,300
A decline of approximately
twenty-four point eight percent.

95
00:05:57,019 --> 00:06:00,644
Comparable-store sales fell
twenty-five point two percent.

96
00:06:01,237 --> 00:06:03,564
The company's gross margin also weakened.

97
00:06:04,134 --> 00:06:08,946
These figures did not isolate the impact
of pricing from every other business

98
00:06:08,986 --> 00:06:09,986
change.

99
00:06:10,256 --> 00:06:13,087
But they revealed the scale of the crisis.

100
00:06:13,726 --> 00:06:19,208
A transformation intended to revive
JCPenney had coincided with one of the most

101
00:06:19,248 --> 00:06:21,598
damaging years in its modern history.

102
00:06:23,432 --> 00:06:28,556
The annual report described declining
store traffic and lower conversion rates.

103
00:06:29,093 --> 00:06:30,093
Fewer visits.

104
00:06:30,626 --> 00:06:31,797
Fewer transactions.

105
00:06:32,641 --> 00:06:33,952
Fewer items sold.

106
00:06:34,445 --> 00:06:38,165
That was particularly dangerous for a
department store chain carrying substantial

107
00:06:38,205 --> 00:06:40,095
fixed operating costs.

108
00:06:40,744 --> 00:06:47,345
A store still requires employees, inventory,
utilities, and physical space even when

109
00:06:47,385 --> 00:06:48,795
customers stop buying.

110
00:06:49,338 --> 00:06:53,800
The new strategy had not simply failed
to create the expected excitement.

111
00:06:54,320 --> 00:06:59,251
The business was losing the sales volume
it needed to support its operations.

112
00:06:59,980 --> 00:07:05,344
And reversing that momentum would require
more than another advertising campaign.

113
00:07:07,041 --> 00:07:10,490
Pricing was only one
part of Johnson's plan.

114
00:07:10,976 --> 00:07:16,201
He wanted JCPenney stores to become
collections of curated branded shops.

115
00:07:16,777 --> 00:07:21,612
He pursued changes to merchandise,
presentation, marketing, and the customer

116
00:07:21,652 --> 00:07:22,652
experience.

117
00:07:23,237 --> 00:07:28,377
The strategy was ambitious because it
attempted to modernize the entire retailer.

118
00:07:28,847 --> 00:07:31,651
But the different initiatives
were interconnected.

119
00:07:32,295 --> 00:07:35,974
Changing merchandise could
unsettle established shoppers.

120
00:07:36,588 --> 00:07:39,857
Changing promotions could alter
their reasons for visiting.

121
00:07:40,340 --> 00:07:43,952
Changing store layouts
required time and investment.

122
00:07:44,405 --> 00:07:49,604
JCPenney was trying to rebuild its identity
while still depending on its existing

123
00:07:49,644 --> 00:07:52,014
customers to fund the transformation.

124
00:07:53,896 --> 00:07:58,642
By 2013, the pressure on
JCPenney's leadership was intense.

125
00:07:59,294 --> 00:08:03,265
The company had already begun
modifying its promotional approach.

126
00:08:03,885 --> 00:08:07,727
Its disappointing results had damaged
confidence in the turnaround.

127
00:08:08,391 --> 00:08:13,380
On April eighth, 2013, Ron Johnson
was replaced as chief executive.

128
00:08:14,019 --> 00:08:18,279
Former CEO Myron Ullman
returned to lead the company.

129
00:08:18,727 --> 00:08:21,864
Johnson's tenure had lasted
less than eighteen months.

130
00:08:22,424 --> 00:08:27,663
The executive brought in to reinvent JCPenney
had departed before his broader store

131
00:08:27,703 --> 00:08:29,553
transformation could be completed.

132
00:08:30,061 --> 00:08:34,810
The question now was whether the company
could win back the customers it had lost.

133
00:08:36,620 --> 00:08:41,412
JCPenney began rebuilding its
promotional relationship with shoppers.

134
00:08:42,073 --> 00:08:46,646
The company restored familiar marketing
approaches and worked to stabilize its

135
00:08:46,686 --> 00:08:47,996
merchandise strategy.

136
00:08:48,484 --> 00:08:52,653
But customer trust and shopping
habits do not return automatically.

137
00:08:53,310 --> 00:08:57,205
The retailer faced the challenge of
recovering sales while dealing with the

138
00:08:57,245 --> 00:09:00,015
financial consequences of
its failed transformation.

139
00:09:00,717 --> 00:09:04,812
And the competitive pressures that
existed before Johnson arrived had not

140
00:09:04,852 --> 00:09:05,852
disappeared.

141
00:09:06,149 --> 00:09:11,290
Department stores still faced changing
consumer behavior and powerful alternatives.

142
00:09:11,881 --> 00:09:16,685
JCPenney had to recover from an internal
strategic shock while continuing to compete

143
00:09:16,725 --> 00:09:18,655
in a difficult industry.

144
00:09:20,339 --> 00:09:25,503
In May 2020, JCPenney filed for
Chapter 11 bankruptcy protection.

145
00:09:26,170 --> 00:09:30,257
The company had struggled for years with
financial pressure and changing retail

146
00:09:30,297 --> 00:09:31,297
conditions.

147
00:09:31,786 --> 00:09:35,730
The COVID-19 pandemic added
another severe disruption.

148
00:09:36,267 --> 00:09:41,220
It would be misleading to claim that Ron
Johnson's pricing strategy alone caused the

149
00:09:41,260 --> 00:09:42,970
bankruptcy seven years later.

150
00:09:43,328 --> 00:09:46,354
The business faced multiple
structural challenges.

151
00:09:46,969 --> 00:09:51,149
But the 2012 collapse remained an
important chapter in its history.

152
00:09:51,533 --> 00:09:56,158
A costly transformation had weakened the
company during a period when it desperately

153
00:09:56,198 --> 00:09:58,548
needed a sustainable path forward.

154
00:10:00,262 --> 00:10:03,349
The evidence points to
four strategic problems.

155
00:10:03,903 --> 00:10:08,301
First, management underestimated how
customers responded to discounts.

156
00:10:08,929 --> 00:10:13,832
Second, it attempted to change deeply
established shopping habits too quickly.

157
00:10:14,348 --> 00:10:19,558
Third, the company transformed pricing,
merchandise, marketing, and stores at the

158
00:10:19,598 --> 00:10:20,598
same time.

159
00:10:21,068 --> 00:10:26,161
And fourth, the scale of the experiment
created enormous financial pressure before

160
00:10:26,201 --> 00:10:28,291
the new model had proved itself.

161
00:10:28,829 --> 00:10:34,121
Fair and Square was not necessarily a
bad idea in every retail environment.

162
00:10:34,627 --> 00:10:39,545
But at JCPenney, the strategy collided
with the expectations of the customers the

163
00:10:39,585 --> 00:10:40,835
company needed most.

164
00:10:42,693 --> 00:10:47,458
Ron Johnson had helped build one of the
most successful retail experiences in the

165
00:10:47,498 --> 00:10:48,445
world.

166
00:10:48,485 --> 00:10:51,175
But JCPenney was not Apple.

167
00:10:51,647 --> 00:10:54,076
Its customers had different expectations.

168
00:10:54,849 --> 00:10:57,197
Its business faced different constraints.

169
00:10:57,971 --> 00:11:03,003
And the habits that brought shoppers through
its doors could not be erased by a new

170
00:11:03,043 --> 00:11:04,413
pricing philosophy.

171
00:11:04,963 --> 00:11:06,967
The lesson is bigger than coupons.

172
00:11:07,781 --> 00:11:12,373
A company can design a strategy that looks
elegant on paper and still misunderstand

173
00:11:12,413 --> 00:11:14,183
the people it serves.

174
00:11:14,749 --> 00:11:18,679
Because changing a business is not
the same as changing its customers.

175
00:11:19,363 --> 00:11:21,790
And every brand leaves evidence.

